WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.064
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.064 --> 00:00:13.958
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Hi, Doug.

00:00:13.958 --> 00:00:16.167
&gt;&gt; Hi, Tracy.
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for coming today.

00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:17.051
&gt;&gt; Thanks for having me.

00:00:17.051 --> 00:00:18.079
This is wonderful.
&gt;&gt; We're looking forward to

00:00:18.079 --> 00:00:19.130
the conversation.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:19.710
&gt;&gt; Happy to be here.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:20.370
This is really nice.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:21.190
Thank you all for being here.

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:22.500
I appreciate it.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.457
&gt;&gt; I was hoping we could start
when you were in our shoes,

00:00:25.457 --> 00:00:28.086
when you were just starting
out on your career,

00:00:28.086 --> 00:00:32.375
your first job out of business school
was as an analyst at American Airlines.

00:00:32.375 --> 00:00:32.934
&gt;&gt; Correct.

00:00:32.934 --> 00:00:37.230
&gt;&gt; You moved around between a couple of
different airline companies after that but

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:38.648
never left the industry.

00:00:38.648 --> 00:00:44.300
What drew you to that first job at
American, and to the airline industry?

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.690
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you're going to test my memory.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:47.295
It's a long time ago.

00:00:47.295 --> 00:00:52.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But no, a lot of people in our industry

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:59.760
that you will find just grew up
with jet fuel in their veins.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.330
Sit and watch airplanes take off when
they were 12 years old, at the airport.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.460
I didn't do any of that stuff.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.958
I was not a huge commercial aviation
enthusiast or anything like it.

00:01:09.958 --> 00:01:12.580
I was looking for a job.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:17.300
I'd gone straight through from
undergrad to graduate school.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.540
My concentration was in finance.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:21.643
I wanted to go somewhere
where finance was important.

00:01:21.643 --> 00:01:26.560
So I looked toward heavily
capitalized industries.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.120
And airlines popped up.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.890
So, as in a number of others, it wasn't so
much airlines, and I was fortunate enough

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:37.470
that, at the time, American Airlines
was trying to build a hub in Nashville,

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:42.830
Tennessee, where I was getting my MBA,
so they came to campus.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.860
I interviewed, and
they invited me back to Dallas.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:46.620
I liked the people there.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.590
It really was as simple as that.

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:54.401
I knew I wanted to go somewhere where what
I was reasonably proficient at mattered.

00:01:54.401 --> 00:01:55.145
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:01:55.145 --> 00:01:58.249
&gt;&gt; So
that was as targeted as I did though, and

00:01:58.249 --> 00:02:01.450
then it was just having done it,
I loved it.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:04.650
It's a fantastic business,
it's really dynamic.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.316
So that's kind of why I stayed in,
it I think.

00:02:06.316 --> 00:02:08.965
But it wasn't anything
more scientific than that.

00:02:08.965 --> 00:02:10.600
&gt;&gt; It makes sense.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.840
But you did stay in the industry.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.450
And many of us,
many of our generation are job hoppers.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:20.990
A lot of us, our next jobs, we won't be
there in more than a couple of years.

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:23.640
What's your advice as someone who
stayed in the same industry for so

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.120
long to people who are thinking
about jumping between companies and

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.820
industries over the course
of their careers?

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:34.780
&gt;&gt; Again, see, all I know is what I did,

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:38.720
so it's hard to suggest that that's
the only way to do it, because it's not.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.260
But what happened with me, again,
is I got into this business.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.690
I found I really enjoyed it.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:50.400
What I like about the airline business

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.910
is that all the disciplines
are really important.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:57.763
I said I wanted to go somewhere finance
matter, but finance does matter, but

00:02:57.763 --> 00:03:02.430
it's a consumer product so
marketing's incredibly important.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.861
There's no more complex
operation in the world so

00:03:05.861 --> 00:03:09.054
logistics is incredibly
important in value.

00:03:09.054 --> 00:03:13.223
The maintenance function is
really a manufacturing function.

00:03:13.223 --> 00:03:16.200
So every discipline's really important.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.360
And how you make all those things
come together is really important.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.839
So what I liked about it is the ability
to gravitate to different Items,

00:03:24.839 --> 00:03:26.910
areas over time.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.736
But so much staying in the industry,
I don't know.

00:03:29.736 --> 00:03:34.320
I know at time,
I contemplated doing other things.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.350
It wasn't that I think no, I need to
stay in this business for any reason.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:42.782
It's just the opportunities that came
around continued to be in the same

00:03:42.782 --> 00:03:43.660
business.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:47.709
Now I don't think I could do anything
else, this is all I've done, but

00:03:47.709 --> 00:03:48.595
I don't know.

00:03:48.595 --> 00:03:53.303
I will say that it's certainly
nice when we bring in people

00:03:53.303 --> 00:03:58.120
from the outside because you
bring in a fresh perspective.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:00.870
And people in our business tend to have

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.880
hold on to some views of the past that
maybe we don't want to hold on to anymore.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.500
So there's always some value in
bringing people in from the outside,

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.600
but there's also a tremendous amount
of value of having people who have

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:14.520
been in the business and know it and
have seen the cycles and understand.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.136
So I think either way,
we need some of each.

00:04:18.136 --> 00:04:23.542
But there certainly is,
if we find someone who is just in for

00:04:23.542 --> 00:04:29.288
a couple years and out,
it's not particularly helpful to us.

00:04:29.288 --> 00:04:33.962
Because it takes longer than that to
really figure out what's going on.

00:04:33.962 --> 00:04:36.548
&gt;&gt; When you first arrived
at this company that has so

00:04:36.548 --> 00:04:39.830
many different disciplines
that are important.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:42.120
&gt;&gt; You hadn't been watching the planes
take off when you were a kid, and

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:45.160
there's a lot of value from
the industry expertise.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:49.140
Just starting out, how did you make a name
for yourself in a company like that?

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.716
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you show up and do your job.

00:04:52.716 --> 00:04:58.781
And try and
make sure that you're being responsive

00:04:58.781 --> 00:05:03.870
to what the organization's looking for.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:08.285
Again, I wasn't incredibly
hard-driving cutthroat,

00:05:08.285 --> 00:05:13.250
I'm going to make sure that I get
this job before someone else does.

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:17.000
But I certainly cared about what I did,
I took it seriously.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.370
And I worked really hard,

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:22.520
I took pride in the work that I did and
people noticed that.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:26.660
And so it wasn't so much that I was
going to figure out how I got to this job

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:31.200
by this time but rather I showed up and
did the job that I was asked to do.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.770
I think I did it pretty well and
people recognized that and

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:36.170
they want you to do more things.

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:41.660
And of course then what really matters is
as you start moving up in an organization,

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:44.650
the skills that might have been
really helpful as an analyst,

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:50.598
are not particularly the ones that
are as important as a manager.

00:05:50.598 --> 00:05:54.440
So you gotta figure out how to adapt and
do those things but anyway,

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:57.320
I tried to do what I was asked to do.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:58.310
I took it really seriously.

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:02.731
I worked pretty hard,
because it mattered to me.

00:06:02.731 --> 00:06:05.425
But if you do that people will find you.

00:06:05.425 --> 00:06:08.735
&gt;&gt; Yeah, were there any key people or
key events that helped you,

00:06:08.735 --> 00:06:11.858
especially when you were moving
up into new positions where

00:06:11.858 --> 00:06:13.930
maybe you didn't have the skills yet?

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:20.230
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, what I remember
from back then where my career

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:25.700
really started to progress little
faster is when I was promoted.

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:30.160
I was a pretty good analyst, but there
were definitely better analysts than me.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:36.290
Smarter, good at Lotus 123, or
whatever the hell we used back then.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:38.958
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so

00:06:38.958 --> 00:06:41.740
anyway, they could build
a spreadsheet faster.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:44.800
I was pretty good but
definitely not the best.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.500
Where my career started progressing
is when I was promoted to

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:51.210
managing a small group of people.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.610
And I've always liked working in teams.

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:56.680
Played team sports growing up.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:03.518
I was comfortable in that environment,
and I quickly realized that if I can just

00:07:03.518 --> 00:07:08.926
get these five people who are smarter
than me to work together,

00:07:08.926 --> 00:07:13.770
we can do a lot more than
the six of us can independently.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:18.430
And I did find that I think
I did that role better

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:20.020
than certainly my peers at the time.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.790
Our team was able to produce more.

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:26.740
I tried to make sure they all got credits,
so then people wanted to be on my team,

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:30.450
and as a result, that's when things
started taking off for me a little more.

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:36.120
&gt;&gt; Interesting, a lot of these dynamics
are the dynamics of a more traditional,

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:40.660
complex company that's been around for
a while and has its hierarchies.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:44.400
A lot of us are looking at jobs in
Silicon Valley, where we move fast and

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:48.195
break things, which isn't necessarily
how I would describe American Airlines.

00:07:48.195 --> 00:07:50.180
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So give us the other perspective.

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:53.755
What can we learn at this point in our
careers from an established company that

00:07:53.755 --> 00:07:55.300
we couldn't learn at a start up?

00:07:55.300 --> 00:08:01.616
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well that's a hard question for
me to answer you.

00:08:01.616 --> 00:08:06.409
Again, back when I came out, there wasn't
an internet and there weren't people,

00:08:06.409 --> 00:08:09.390
the best and
brightest didn't go work at start ups.

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:11.959
They went and worked at established firms.

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.622
So it's what I knew.

00:08:14.622 --> 00:08:17.870
But look, the basic principles.

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:21.000
I'm certain, are the same.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.440
That is leadership skills.

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:25.730
Getting people to work as a unit.

00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:30.210
Communicating with people in a way that
they understand what the mission of

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:32.090
the organization is.

00:08:32.090 --> 00:08:36.800
In a way that they feel
motivated to produce.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:39.760
That's how you're successful
in a large organization.

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:42.758
I can't imagine that's not how you're
successful in a smaller organization.

00:08:42.758 --> 00:08:48.890
And look, the large organization
stuff may sound old and stodgy.

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:57.220
I think the reason our team
has done as well as we have,

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:01.220
given the cards we were handed anyway,
is because

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:06.520
I think we think much more like a startup,
that startup mentality.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:07.970
We took risk others wouldn't take.

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:10.040
We didn't things people
thought were crazy.

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:11.360
And there's so

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:15.140
much value to be created if you're willing
to do that in large organizations.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:20.982
Because the organization doesn't,
it will reward behavior,

00:09:20.982 --> 00:09:24.800
but it doesn't encourage the behavior.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:29.108
If, anyway, any of you,
if asked look, this is,

00:09:29.108 --> 00:09:33.117
I'll give you a roll a dice,
if it comes up six,

00:09:33.117 --> 00:09:38.180
I'll give you a $50, but
it comes didn't come as six.

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:39.250
You gotta pay me five.

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:40.200
Well, I gotta well, of course.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:43.890
You give me ten to one odds in
something that's one in six.

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:47.260
If you mention that to your boss in
a large organization, I got this idea,

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:51.970
the boss will say wait a second,
you're telling me five out of six times.

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:53.320
It's not going to work.

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:55.170
I'm going to look bad
five out of six times.

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:55.839
We're not doing that.

00:09:57.260 --> 00:10:00.650
So, those of us who are willing to say,
what are you talking about?

00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:03.550
I don't care.
It's a great bet, we're going to do that.

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:07.250
Do extremely well because the risk
reward relationship works of course

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:08.740
like it does everywhere else.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:11.940
And you're in an environment
where risk is less encouraged.

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:14.770
So the reward is

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:18.370
much higher than it would be if everyone
was taking the same level of risk.

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:19.430
Or had the same attitude.

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:23.840
So look,
again I only know what I've been through.

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:27.770
But I don't feel like it's been boring and
stodgy.

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:30.240
It's been incredibly dynamic and exciting.

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:36.010
And the fun part is taking these risks and
seeing, and when other's don't.

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:39.220
And having some things that others
would look at and think of God,

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:44.060
that didn't work for you,
which we say it's exactly the way.

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:46.500
I would never,
I would do the exact same thing.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:49.150
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Because it was the right decision.

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:50.200
Just because it came up too,

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:52.910
doesn't mean it was the wrong
decision to take that back.

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:54.570
It was the right decision.

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:56.710
So, and
as long as you keep doing those things,

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:59.270
particularly in an organization where
you're not competing with a lot of people

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:02.290
doing the same thing,
you can do really well.

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:05.380
&gt;&gt; And
is this perspective you've developed about

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:09.000
being willing to take the risk, has this
developed and changed over your career?

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:11.030
&gt;&gt; I don't think so.

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:11.690
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:12.940
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I don't know.

00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:16.275
I used to like going to Vegas
a lot when I was younger.

00:11:16.275 --> 00:11:19.110
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, and, look, I've,

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:25.096
I'm a big proponent of risk taking,
and always have been.

00:11:25.096 --> 00:11:29.854
So, and like I say,
it's helped me a lot, and

00:11:29.854 --> 00:11:35.110
things like just in
the introduction of my career.

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:38.850
When I left,
this is inside airline stuff, so sorry.

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:45.617
But you know, back when I left American
Airlines in, whatever that was, 1991.

00:11:45.617 --> 00:11:48.003
American Airlines,
that was the place to be.

00:11:48.003 --> 00:11:50.080
We hired 40 amazing year.

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:52.710
We were the best and the brightest.

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:56.910
No one went to work for Northwest
Airlines, for God sakes, from American,

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:57.630
but I did.

00:11:57.630 --> 00:11:58.575
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:11:58.575 --> 00:12:05.520
&gt;&gt; Because I could do a lot more at
Northwest than I could at American.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:09.980
So anyway,
I would encourage people to go take risks.

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:13.900
That's what I've been doing for
a long time.

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:15.240
&gt;&gt; Good.

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:16.570
Since the Vegas days.

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:17.790
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:21.080
&gt;&gt; But
combined with your risk taking style.

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:24.180
There's also a lot out there who say
you have a leadership style that's

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:25.150
very humble.

00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:27.120
Very confident but very humble.

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:31.450
And the airline industry has a lot
of folks with some pretty big egos.

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:32.500
&gt;&gt; They've got confidence.

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:34.570
&gt;&gt; Yes,
yes everyone's got the confidence, but

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:36.780
the humility I think is
the distinctive feature.

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:42.610
What are the trade offs between the two
different styles when you're running such

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:43.930
a complex organization?

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:47.100
So many different people to communicate
with as you expressed before?

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:49.435
&gt;&gt; Okay, well thanks for
the characterization.

00:12:49.435 --> 00:12:50.170
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I appreciate that.

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:51.860
I hope it's true.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:58.490
What I know is that, this is huge

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:03.400
team sport and there's no individual that
makes a difference and the way it makes

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:06.450
a difference is if you get the whole team
pulling together and working together.

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:12.720
So I'm stunned sometimes to see how
some individuals try and position

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:18.330
themselves because I can't imagine what
it's like to work for that person.

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:21.000
When you're he one doing all the work and
they're taking all the credit.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:23.945
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So I try not to do that.

00:13:23.945 --> 00:13:27.330
It's hard by the way because
people want to do that.

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:34.360
Media only wants to talk to me and
everyone

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:38.070
wants to try and simplify it into you made
this decision, you made this decision.

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:39.647
And I didn't make those decisions.

00:13:39.647 --> 00:13:40.160
Somebody else made those decisions.

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:41.973
I was just, I said okay.

00:13:41.973 --> 00:13:47.818
So at any rate, I don't think it's
a conscious attempt to try and

00:13:47.818 --> 00:13:50.380
be different than anybody.

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:51.820
It's just who we are.

00:13:51.820 --> 00:13:55.940
And what we've learned long ago is that
if we all work together as a team and

00:13:55.940 --> 00:13:58.140
stick together as a team,
we can do really great things.

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:01.620
But if any one of us tries to make it
about us, then it starts to fall apart.

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:02.920
So that's worked for us.

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:05.260
Other ways work for other people.

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:06.420
That's just what works for us.

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:07.320
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:11.720
Building on that, the airline industry
is one that invokes a lot of negative

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:13.390
sentiment among customers sometimes.

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:14.000
&gt;&gt; Really?

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:17.330
&gt;&gt; The Wall Street Journal,
I've heard it mentioned.

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:22.320
The Wall Street Journal recently published
a report showing that American Airlines

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:26.260
was ranked last in sort of overall
ranking of US airlines for 2015.

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:27.945
&gt;&gt; I didn't read that one.

00:14:27.945 --> 00:14:29.870
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I'll send it to you.

00:14:29.870 --> 00:14:31.560
&gt;&gt; Sure it's wrong.

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:32.270
&gt;&gt; Probably.

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:32.970
Probably.

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:33.473
Just a slip up.

00:14:33.473 --> 00:14:35.420
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; Maybe I was looking at it upside down.

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:38.006
But.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:14:38.006 --> 00:14:42.220
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE] [COUGH]

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:46.510
Well what I want to know is in a team
sport and you're the team captain and

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:50.010
there's result like this,
how do you lead the team?

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:52.210
&gt;&gt; Well you understand the results and

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:57.160
make sure, look I did read the article and
you didn't read it upside down.

00:14:58.480 --> 00:14:59.300
But look,

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:02.990
we are in the middle of integration
during the time that was measured.

00:15:02.990 --> 00:15:06.240
So things like what they
measured are important.

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:09.870
But some of the things they
measured are really hard to do,

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:14.194
when you are running two separate airline,
like baggage connections.

00:15:14.194 --> 00:15:16.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; When we've got people connecting from

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:20.600
US Airways to American Airlines,
it's harder.

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:22.880
All that, we are well aware of that.

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:27.500
That's the problem with integration
we are that stuffs fixed.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:29.160
We won't be last next year.

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:31.350
American Airlines is one airline now.

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:35.410
And first and
foremost that's the answer, but

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:40.284
to your broader,
that specific set of circumstances but

00:15:40.284 --> 00:15:44.549
I'm not about to suggest
we do everything great,

00:15:44.549 --> 00:15:49.442
it's a great business and
people shouldn't complain.

00:15:49.442 --> 00:15:54.314
It's a difficult function to
transport people through bad

00:15:54.314 --> 00:15:58.610
weather like we're about
to have in the East coast.

00:15:58.610 --> 00:16:02.937
And people fail out for the product and
there's, if we do everything perfectly,

00:16:02.937 --> 00:16:06.763
if we do or just perfectly well then
The air press perfectly maintained,

00:16:06.763 --> 00:16:11.330
everyone shows up on time, everyone
does their job exactly as they should.

00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:15.050
The best we're going to do,
in terms of on time performance,

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:19.131
over the course of a year anyway,
is probably around 85%.

00:16:19.131 --> 00:16:23.470
85% of the time you should
expect at the very,

00:16:23.470 --> 00:16:27.820
very best run airline that you're going to
arrive within, by the way the definition

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:31.835
of on time is within 14 minutes of your
scheduled arrival time, which is nice too.

00:16:31.835 --> 00:16:35.001
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's our government, but

00:16:35.001 --> 00:16:40.635
none the less,
what other business has 15% failure rates?

00:16:40.635 --> 00:16:42.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:45.770
&gt;&gt; And for a product, but
that's entirely due to weather.

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:48.231
In the example I just gave,
there's no maintenance, cancellations.

00:16:48.231 --> 00:16:51.060
50% of our flights get
delayed because of weather.

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:56.150
So we have this challenge of
a product that is a means to an end.

00:16:56.150 --> 00:16:58.800
Most people don't buy airline tickets
to fly around in an airplane,

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:00.040
they're trying to get somewhere else.

00:17:01.070 --> 00:17:06.627
And is also because of how complicated
it is, and how capital intensive it is.

00:17:06.627 --> 00:17:10.127
It's reasonably expensive, so they expect
it to work as a means to an end, and

00:17:10.127 --> 00:17:11.730
when it doesn't, they get upset.

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:15.115
Now our challenge is to make sure
that we manage that as well, and

00:17:15.115 --> 00:17:17.370
we're getting much better at this.

00:17:17.370 --> 00:17:21.140
When things do go wrong, make sure that we
were able to respond much more quickly and

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:23.300
get people where they need to go.

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:25.100
because people do understand weather.

00:17:25.100 --> 00:17:28.060
But they still want to get
where they were trying to go.

00:17:28.060 --> 00:17:32.350
Anyway, I've already forget what the
question was, Gracie, but we work really

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:38.920
hard in a difficult job to do it
really well and to focus on customers.

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:40.500
We have a phenomenal team of people.

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:45.730
We got over 100,000 employees who are just
out there doing a great job everyday,

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:48.260
it's a really noble thing they do.

00:17:48.260 --> 00:17:51.810
They get beat up sometimes because they're
the ones standing there when the plane

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:56.585
can't go, but the reality is,
those people,

00:17:56.585 --> 00:18:01.520
they make commerce run, business couldn't
operate without the airline industry.

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:05.670
They get people to go see other people,
they get people to go see the world.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:09.439
And it all happens because of the work
they do and we're really proud of it.

00:18:09.439 --> 00:18:14.244
But we know we have to get
better at reaccommodating

00:18:14.244 --> 00:18:17.046
people when things go wrong.

00:18:17.046 --> 00:18:17.884
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:18:17.884 --> 00:18:21.452
And related to things beyond your control,
ten days

00:18:21.452 --> 00:18:25.840
after you became CEO of US Airways
&gt;&gt; You woke up to the news that

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:28.670
two planes had crashed into
the World Trade Center.

00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:32.680
What went through your head at that
moment and what did you do that morning?

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:35.460
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I was in Phoenix at the time,
working for America West Airlines.

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:39.490
Yeah, I was the CEO for ten days.

00:18:39.490 --> 00:18:41.404
It was a nice ten days.

00:18:41.404 --> 00:18:42.567
&gt;&gt; And then-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:18:42.567 --> 00:18:43.637
&gt;&gt; It's all relative.

00:18:43.637 --> 00:18:50.354
&gt;&gt; So look, the immediate concern of
course was for the safety of our team, so

00:18:50.354 --> 00:18:56.550
quickly after learning about
the flights at the World Trade Center.

00:18:56.550 --> 00:18:57.270
My phone rang and

00:18:57.270 --> 00:19:00.930
it was the FAA was grounding
the entire commercial airline fleet.

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:03.910
And so we were putting airplanes down.

00:19:03.910 --> 00:19:08.740
So the best message I got was hearing that
everyone of our airplanes was safe and

00:19:08.740 --> 00:19:10.460
they were on the ground, so.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:14.040
But anyway,
it quickly turns into the next three days,

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:16.280
trying to shut down airspace and
those things.

00:19:16.280 --> 00:19:21.100
But not much longer, once you get
past the immediate safety concerns.

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:23.400
And by the way, extreme empathy for

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:27.480
those who didn't have their employees
safe at American and United.

00:19:27.480 --> 00:19:32.050
But shortly thereafter, we moved into,

00:19:32.050 --> 00:19:35.270
what are we going to do about the longterm
viability of this business and this, and

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:38.710
particularly the company that I was
running which was America West Airlines?

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:45.540
We had a range to financing
with some suppliers of ours,

00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:49.040
Airbus and GE, to help get us through
what was already a tough time.

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:54.410
It was arranged but
we have not fully executed.

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:56.740
So that of course, fell through.

00:19:56.740 --> 00:20:00.680
We found ourselves in a position where we
absolutely in that environment did not

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:03.235
have enough cash to make
it through the winter.

00:20:03.235 --> 00:20:06.800
We were going to go, and in that
environment, since there was no way,

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:11.210
even through a bankruptcy filing
to expect things like dip loans

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:14.790
that would allow you to survive,
we were going to shut the company down.

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:18.460
We were going to liquidate without
some sort of capital infusion.

00:20:18.460 --> 00:20:22.449
So and the only possible way to get
a capital infusion in that environment was

00:20:22.449 --> 00:20:23.520
the US Government.

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:28.855
They established this
Government Loan Guarantee Program so

00:20:28.855 --> 00:20:33.990
I went from,
when I took the job in September of 2001.

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:37.360
The one thing I remember telling the board
is look, I can do all this stuff.

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:42.110
I've done operations, I've done the
marquee side, I've done the finance side.

00:20:42.110 --> 00:20:45.170
The only thing I really haven't done
is government affairs, but that's okay,

00:20:45.170 --> 00:20:46.560
because we're this little
airline in Phoenix and

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:48.420
we never had to do anything
with Washington, really.

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:50.570
It's more the bigger
airlines mess with that.

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:55.355
Well, I was found myself living
in Washington DC, lobbying for

00:20:55.355 --> 00:20:58.733
a government loan for
the next three months.

00:20:58.733 --> 00:20:59.617
&gt;&gt; Crash course.

00:20:59.617 --> 00:21:00.873
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:21:00.873 --> 00:21:02.730
&gt;&gt; How did you communicate?

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:05.910
So you had a lot of communications with
the government the month that followed.

00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:09.310
How did you think about the various
communications to the public,

00:21:09.310 --> 00:21:12.190
to customers, employees, shareholders.

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:15.050
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we tried to make them
think everything was okay.

00:21:15.050 --> 00:21:18.539
Even though we weren't quite sure it was,
we were honest.

00:21:18.539 --> 00:21:21.077
Let everybody know that we were
going to get this done even though we

00:21:21.077 --> 00:21:22.161
weren't certain we were.

00:21:22.161 --> 00:21:26.973
So the communication was important, but

00:21:26.973 --> 00:21:35.170
the real important part was trying
to get through the loan process.

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:37.530
It's by the way,
I'm going to embarrass somebody.

00:21:37.530 --> 00:21:39.180
Is Marlena Sloick in here?

00:21:40.290 --> 00:21:41.510
Hi.

00:21:41.510 --> 00:21:44.260
Her father John Sloick was our
investment banker through all of this.

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:49.481
He lived through this with me, so anyway.

00:21:49.481 --> 00:21:51.155
&gt;&gt; Good to see you.

00:21:51.155 --> 00:21:51.946
Thanks for coming.

00:21:51.946 --> 00:21:52.598
&gt;&gt; Nice to see you.

00:21:52.598 --> 00:21:53.809
Thanks.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:21:53.809 --> 00:21:54.925
&gt;&gt; Tell your dad hi.

00:21:54.925 --> 00:21:56.857
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So anyway, he and

00:21:56.857 --> 00:21:58.120
I went through a lot together.

00:21:58.120 --> 00:22:02.670
But I want to give you one quick
story which I think can help

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:04.730
pull this together and
also this career stuff.

00:22:04.730 --> 00:22:07.850
because seeing Marlena
reminded me of this.

00:22:07.850 --> 00:22:13.100
We went through this process and
we were told by the government, no.

00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:14.330
We were told.

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:23.100
Marlena's father and I sat there and
had the Department of the Treasury

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:28.290
send us a fax at Citi's government
affairs office Friday at 5 PM,

00:22:28.290 --> 00:22:33.500
so they could then leave,
that said we've reviewed your file,

00:22:33.500 --> 00:22:39.140
and need to tell you that we cannot
approve your application at this time.

00:22:39.140 --> 00:22:40.500
Which is no.

00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:43.350
And it's the worst day of my life.

00:22:43.350 --> 00:22:47.760
We were going to liquidate, and I didn't
know what we were going to do about it.

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:52.018
And again, from this time now, so it's
now, I'm the CEO of this airline, I've

00:22:52.018 --> 00:22:56.500
been CEO, this is now October, November,
so I've been seeing them for two months.

00:22:56.500 --> 00:22:57.336
&gt;&gt; Very experienced.

00:22:57.336 --> 00:22:58.266
&gt;&gt; Yeah, exactly.

00:22:58.266 --> 00:23:03.486
So I just can't help but think,
this didn't turn out right.

00:23:03.486 --> 00:23:07.433
My career, I'm shortly going to
be the worst CEO of all time.

00:23:07.433 --> 00:23:10.040
[COUGH] I was CEO for two months and
we liquidated the company.

00:23:10.040 --> 00:23:11.210
What am I going to do?

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:13.750
And all this kind of me, me, me stuff.

00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:16.840
[COUGH]
Which again,

00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:18.438
I don't feel badly about that.

00:23:18.438 --> 00:23:19.136
That's natural.

00:23:19.136 --> 00:23:21.615
You get into jobs and
you kind of worry about your career and

00:23:21.615 --> 00:23:24.108
I was still worried about my career and
what I was going to do.

00:23:24.108 --> 00:23:28.400
And here I knew this company
was going to liquidate.

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:30.760
And here is the career story.

00:23:30.760 --> 00:23:37.810
I go and
on that flight home from DC to Phoenix.

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:40.640
I always get up and talk to our team and
crew, because it's important and

00:23:40.640 --> 00:23:41.460
I learn from them.

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:46.120
But I didn't want to go talk to the flight
crew, because this is front page news.

00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:49.640
No one knew yet about this letter,
but everyone was asking so

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:50.530
I knew they were going to ask.

00:23:50.530 --> 00:23:52.790
And I knew I couldn't lie
to the flight attendants.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:58.230
So I was contemplating just spending
this four hours in my seat.

00:23:58.230 --> 00:24:01.730
But then I thought I can't do that,
I gotta get up, so I go back and talk and

00:24:01.730 --> 00:24:02.535
I'm glad I did.

00:24:02.535 --> 00:24:07.081
This flight attendant in the back asked me
what's going on, I tell her, well, gee,

00:24:07.081 --> 00:24:11.376
it doesn't look to good right now, I just
We're fighting through it but I just,

00:24:11.376 --> 00:24:15.190
I don't feel good about it and she says,
well what's that going to mean?

00:24:15.190 --> 00:24:18.360
I said, well look if we can't get this
done, you know, we'll file bankruptcy and

00:24:18.360 --> 00:24:21.180
we'll try and hang on but
I don't feel good about that either and

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:24.030
she just looks at me and
says you can't do that.

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:29.950
I've been doing this job for ten years,
I'm a single mom, I've got my

00:24:29.950 --> 00:24:34.090
life arranged around this flight
attendant job, I can't do anything else,

00:24:34.090 --> 00:24:36.920
I'm really good at it, which, by the way,
she was, because I'd seen her.

00:24:36.920 --> 00:24:38.396
And you just can't let that happen.

00:24:38.396 --> 00:24:43.840
And I'm like, [LAUGH] So

00:24:43.840 --> 00:24:46.370
okay, but no, it was really humble.

00:24:46.370 --> 00:24:49.950
So I went back, sat in my seat,
pulled out the damn letter again,

00:24:49.950 --> 00:24:54.890
and realized that it said at this time,
cannot prove it at this time.

00:24:54.890 --> 00:24:58.930
So okay, I landed that night,
we went straight to work.

00:24:58.930 --> 00:24:59.800
We worked all weekend.

00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:03.319
We showed back up in DC Monday morning,
John with us.

00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:05.980
With a new application.

00:25:05.980 --> 00:25:08.700
And these guys in treasury said,
what are you doing here?

00:25:08.700 --> 00:25:10.180
You said not this time.

00:25:10.180 --> 00:25:11.310
Here's another application.

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:14.330
We're not going away.

00:25:14.330 --> 00:25:16.580
And we kept fighting.

00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:17.380
And we didn't give up.

00:25:17.380 --> 00:25:24.358
&gt;From that time, that airline, that little
Ameriquest airline had to have that loan.

00:25:24.358 --> 00:25:29.160
That little airline American West Airlines
lived off of wage advantages.

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:32.025
Other airlines went through bankruptcy and
got their wages down to ours.

00:25:32.025 --> 00:25:33.350
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; So all of the sudden we had a new

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:35.690
problem, which was we didn't have the
revenue generating capability they did,

00:25:35.690 --> 00:25:37.485
but they had the same
cost structure as us now.

00:25:37.485 --> 00:25:38.660
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:25:38.660 --> 00:25:39.520
So we had to do a merger.

00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:42.330
So we merged with US Airways.

00:25:42.330 --> 00:25:44.836
And then US Airways ended up with
essentially the same kind of problems so

00:25:44.836 --> 00:25:46.298
we ended up merging with American.

00:25:46.298 --> 00:25:50.870
All those things,
none of those, from the time of

00:25:50.870 --> 00:25:55.100
that encounter was I spending time
thinking what's best for my career.

00:25:55.100 --> 00:25:59.670
Every one of them was, I'm responsible for
these hard working people.

00:25:59.670 --> 00:26:04.030
Who, if we don't do our jobs right,
aren't going to have a job.

00:26:04.030 --> 00:26:05.490
And it's not their fault.

00:26:05.490 --> 00:26:07.750
These people are fantastic.

00:26:07.750 --> 00:26:10.750
They're depending upon us to make sure
that we put the company in a position

00:26:10.750 --> 00:26:12.700
where they don't have to
worry about whether or

00:26:12.700 --> 00:26:14.150
not the company's going to be there for
them.

00:26:14.150 --> 00:26:16.270
So the reason they work so
hard for those mergers,

00:26:16.270 --> 00:26:19.260
and the reason I personally work so
hard on it was not because we wanted to

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:23.230
get ourselves in a position of being CEO
of the largest airline in the world,

00:26:23.230 --> 00:26:26.670
is because that was the only way to
get that group of people safe harbor.

00:26:26.670 --> 00:26:31.450
Anyway, and so the moral of the story is,
from the time I stopped thinking about my

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:35.760
career and
started thinking about doing my job for

00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:38.930
a bigger cause, my career took off.

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:41.110
And so anyway, I like that part.

00:26:41.110 --> 00:26:44.501
So my message of this is, it's hard to do.

00:26:44.501 --> 00:26:48.370
[COUGH] You always hear people
say go do what you love.

00:26:49.700 --> 00:26:51.110
To which I thought, that's BS.

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:55.275
I love playing basketball, but, [COUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:26:55.275 --> 00:26:56.570
&gt;&gt; [COUGH]

00:26:56.570 --> 00:27:00.669
Nobody's going to pay me to do that, but

00:27:00.669 --> 00:27:05.920
I think what really is true,
if you can figure out a way to make what

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:11.280
you do be bigger than just you,
that'll work out really well for you.

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:12.900
And again, it's not that easy.

00:27:12.900 --> 00:27:15.140
It's a lot like saying, do what you love.

00:27:15.140 --> 00:27:19.380
But if you can find a way,
once you find that, don't let go of it,

00:27:19.380 --> 00:27:23.130
because that's when things
really start happening.

00:27:23.130 --> 00:27:27.100
When you're not just, because look, if it
had been me, if I had been worried, gee,

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:31.700
I want to be CEO of American Airlines,
at some point during this whole fight,

00:27:31.700 --> 00:27:33.960
I would have said,
CEO of US Airways isn't that bad.

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:34.810
I'm fine.

00:27:34.810 --> 00:27:36.030
I'm doing fine.

00:27:36.030 --> 00:27:37.110
I don't have to move.

00:27:37.110 --> 00:27:39.150
This was okay.

00:27:39.150 --> 00:27:42.080
But that never crossed our minds,
because this was, no, no, no,

00:27:42.080 --> 00:27:45.890
we gotta get this done for
all these people that are counting on us.

00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:46.970
So.

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:50.230
And I'm glad you bring up the mergers
because I wanted to ask before

00:27:50.230 --> 00:27:54.040
we talk about the success of the US
Airways American merger about what you

00:27:54.040 --> 00:27:57.110
learned from the failed attempts to
merge with United and Continental.

00:27:58.140 --> 00:27:59.660
&gt;&gt; They weren't failures.

00:27:59.660 --> 00:28:01.090
&gt;&gt; Good, I'm already learning.

00:28:01.090 --> 00:28:03.171
&gt;&gt; You're reading it upside-down again.

00:28:03.171 --> 00:28:07.690
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And, look, this is back to the risk

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:13.010
thing, and of course,
this is perceived as failure.

00:28:13.010 --> 00:28:16.700
But I'm really glad we did it.

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:21.856
The first one that Gracie is referring to
was shortly after doing the America West,

00:28:21.856 --> 00:28:26.510
US Airways merger, and
we were actually shorter than this part,

00:28:26.510 --> 00:28:28.920
we were just one year into it.

00:28:28.920 --> 00:28:33.090
Delta Airlines was in bankruptcy,
they were about to emerge from bankruptcy.

00:28:33.090 --> 00:28:35.740
We came up with a great idea

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:38.850
bringing them out of bankruptcy with
a merger with the then-US Airways,

00:28:38.850 --> 00:28:41.920
which hadn't even really merged
with America West very well yet.

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:43.140
But it was a good idea.

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:44.800
It would have been a really good airline.

00:28:46.020 --> 00:28:49.325
And the Delta team didn't like it,
so it turned into a hostile.

00:28:49.325 --> 00:28:54.870
[COUGH] Which doesn't happen very often,
hostiles of companies in bankruptcy.

00:28:54.870 --> 00:28:56.600
But we tried it.

00:28:56.600 --> 00:28:58.820
We knew going in it was high risk and

00:28:58.820 --> 00:29:04.258
probably more likely than not not to work
but it was rolling the six on the die.

00:29:04.258 --> 00:29:07.800
Huge upside [COUGH] if it worked,
excuse me.

00:29:07.800 --> 00:29:11.010
And if it didn't work, it didn't work.

00:29:11.010 --> 00:29:13.210
And also, if it didn't work,
what we knew is,

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:17.490
we were going to, they would have
to figure out something else to do.

00:29:17.490 --> 00:29:18.370
And they did.

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:22.710
What happened is, long story short, that
group of people when you're in bankruptcy,

00:29:22.710 --> 00:29:24.120
you don't have a board,
you have creditors.

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:24.670
Well, you have a board.

00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:29.470
But the people running the company are the
people that you stiffed, so the people

00:29:29.470 --> 00:29:34.100
they owe money that aren't getting paid
are now in control of the company.

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:37.610
And those people who you are appealing to
by saying, look, you'll get much more of

00:29:37.610 --> 00:29:42.520
your money back if you do this with us
than with them, kind of believed us but

00:29:42.520 --> 00:29:44.880
at the end said,
look we're going to stick with them.

00:29:44.880 --> 00:29:47.930
But they insisted that,
that board be changed.

00:29:47.930 --> 00:29:51.460
That board put people on
that were pro-consolidation,

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:55.000
including Richard Anderson,
who now runs the place.

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:58.100
And shortly after the merger,
that Northwest,

00:29:58.100 --> 00:29:59.990
that Delta went and
did a merger with Northwest.

00:29:59.990 --> 00:30:03.160
I don't think that would have
happened if we had done the hostile.

00:30:03.160 --> 00:30:06.850
And if Delta hadn't merged with Northwest,

00:30:06.850 --> 00:30:12.490
then our next aborted attempt that
we failed at was trying to then,

00:30:12.490 --> 00:30:15.480
after that happened,
merge US Airways and United.

00:30:15.480 --> 00:30:18.195
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
And we got pretty close there but

00:30:18.195 --> 00:30:21.865
United eventually told us no and
picked Continental instead.

00:30:21.865 --> 00:30:24.645
So United merged with Continental, another
thing that wouldn't have happened had we

00:30:24.645 --> 00:30:26.425
not tried to get the first one done.

00:30:26.425 --> 00:30:29.740
So anyway, I was being flippant
with you saying we didn't fail.

00:30:29.740 --> 00:30:32.180
&gt;From the outside those are failures.

00:30:32.180 --> 00:30:33.740
And that's getting back to my point.

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:34.630
I don't feel badly about them at all.

00:30:34.630 --> 00:30:37.250
And people would write at the time,

00:30:37.250 --> 00:30:41.630
gee, again putting it all on me,
Doug failed at this merger attempt.

00:30:41.630 --> 00:30:43.840
But it didn't do exactly what we wanted.

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:46.210
We wanted them both to work, but

00:30:46.210 --> 00:30:48.820
it progressed us to where we
could do what we needed to do.

00:30:48.820 --> 00:30:50.800
And it moved the industry
where it needed to move and

00:30:50.800 --> 00:30:54.530
there was no one else out there
trying to make that happen.

00:30:54.530 --> 00:30:57.230
I don't know where we'd be if
we hadn't tried do those things.

00:30:57.230 --> 00:31:00.610
&gt;&gt; And so with American,
I think one of the reasons it was so

00:31:00.610 --> 00:31:03.280
successful was you built this strong
relationship with the labor unions.

00:31:04.310 --> 00:31:07.640
Can you talk about the freights and

00:31:07.640 --> 00:31:10.680
what about you as a leader made
those relationships so successful?

00:31:10.680 --> 00:31:12.290
&gt;&gt; Well again, not all me.

00:31:12.290 --> 00:31:19.550
A really good team of
people who cared about so

00:31:19.550 --> 00:31:24.480
much about taking care of our team that
we knew that we could do so much for

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:28.290
them that we also knew is going to be
best for the employees of American.

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:35.208
So, but we did the, rather non-traditional
step of while American was in bankruptcy,

00:31:35.208 --> 00:31:40.790
beginning negotiations,
[LAUGH] so, we're at US Airways,

00:31:40.790 --> 00:31:44.300
we started negotiating with
the American Airlines unions for

00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:47.490
a contract that we would be willing
to sign, if indeed a merger got done.

00:31:47.490 --> 00:31:50.770
These people didn't work for us,
[LAUGH] they worked for American.

00:31:50.770 --> 00:31:54.420
And we're meeting with them in Phoenix and
signing contracts with them.

00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:55.820
Which, look, I didn't do lightly.

00:31:55.820 --> 00:32:00.032
I know it offended my friends in American
that we were meeting with their employees.

00:32:00.032 --> 00:32:03.864
But again,
we had to get this done for our team.

00:32:03.864 --> 00:32:05.106
It was the right thing to do.

00:32:05.106 --> 00:32:07.658
So to your point, we recognize and

00:32:07.658 --> 00:32:11.270
we learned it in the Delta
transaction frankly.

00:32:11.270 --> 00:32:14.530
We did have the creditors on
board in the Delta transaction.

00:32:14.530 --> 00:32:17.310
What we didn't get was
the employees on board.

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:18.760
They were very proud of Delta.

00:32:18.760 --> 00:32:21.460
They wanted to stay with Delta and
that hurt us.

00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:24.980
So we recognized we needed
to get the team on board.

00:32:24.980 --> 00:32:27.610
So we approached American's employees.

00:32:27.610 --> 00:32:30.950
We laid out for
them what we planned to do.

00:32:30.950 --> 00:32:32.530
We told them why we thought it was better.

00:32:32.530 --> 00:32:35.960
They agreed, and it wouldn't
have gotten done with out them..

00:32:37.730 --> 00:32:39.690
It worked out well.

00:32:39.690 --> 00:32:44.595
&gt;&gt; And now that the merger is successful,
your the CEO of the largest airline in

00:32:44.595 --> 00:32:48.359
the world,
120,000 employees around the globe.

00:32:48.359 --> 00:32:50.298
You're also the father of three.

00:32:50.298 --> 00:32:50.972
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:32:50.972 --> 00:32:53.882
&gt;&gt; How does being a CEO
compare to being a father?

00:32:53.882 --> 00:32:57.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Father's a lot harder.

00:32:57.440 --> 00:32:59.750
I don't know.

00:32:59.750 --> 00:33:00.630
I have great kids.

00:33:00.630 --> 00:33:06.850
I try really hard to be
a father more than a CEO.

00:33:06.850 --> 00:33:11.140
My job, I probably spend more
hours at CEO, unfortunately.

00:33:11.140 --> 00:33:15.680
But frankly, I learned so

00:33:15.680 --> 00:33:20.227
much from being a father
that I can use at work.

00:33:20.227 --> 00:33:23.340
[COUGH]
Anyway,

00:33:23.340 --> 00:33:26.170
raising children is this amazing thing.

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:32.320
Everybody does it at some point so
it's not like I'm an expert here.

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:35.580
But just watching the development and
watching how people interact and

00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:39.330
watching how your kids interact
helps me a lot to understand and

00:33:39.330 --> 00:33:42.620
to actually go manage the company.

00:33:42.620 --> 00:33:47.630
So I learned a lot more about, I've
become a better CEO because I'm a father.

00:33:47.630 --> 00:33:50.670
I try really hard to be
a good father as well.

00:33:50.670 --> 00:33:53.560
I do things to make sure that
I'm home as much as I can be.

00:33:53.560 --> 00:33:57.800
This is a rarity for
me where I'm out of town for

00:33:57.800 --> 00:34:00.711
a night, and it's not required work,
but I wanted do it.

00:34:00.711 --> 00:34:01.350
&gt;&gt; Appreciate it.

00:34:01.350 --> 00:34:02.810
&gt;&gt; Well, I wanted to do it for George.

00:34:04.190 --> 00:34:06.432
And I appreciate it as well.

00:34:06.432 --> 00:34:10.250
So I try and stay home as much as I can.

00:34:10.250 --> 00:34:11.540
My job requires me to travel.

00:34:11.540 --> 00:34:17.050
I refuse to do,
I almost never do work dinners in Dallas.

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:21.180
If I'm going to be home,
I'm going to have a meal with my kids.

00:34:21.180 --> 00:34:23.780
If somebody wants to meet with me, of
course, that's important to the company.

00:34:23.780 --> 00:34:27.130
We can do it at lunch or breakfast,
or we can do it at my office.

00:34:27.130 --> 00:34:30.260
But there's no reason for
me to be going to a dinner in Dallas.

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:31.680
So I have rules like that.

00:34:31.680 --> 00:34:34.964
That I just make sure that I stay engaged,
stay balanced.

00:34:34.964 --> 00:34:38.803
&gt;&gt; When you moved to Dallas after
the acquisition was that a family

00:34:38.803 --> 00:34:42.860
conversation about how you balance
where the kids have been raised

00:34:42.860 --> 00:34:45.376
versus where you're doing work?

00:34:45.376 --> 00:34:45.887
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:34:45.887 --> 00:34:49.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:34:49.440 --> 00:34:50.426
&gt;&gt; You're good at this.

00:34:50.426 --> 00:34:55.245
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So do you want to hear this story?

00:34:55.245 --> 00:34:55.970
&gt;&gt; I do.

00:34:55.970 --> 00:34:57.020
&gt;&gt; Okay, sorry.

00:34:57.020 --> 00:34:58.670
You know, it's horrible.

00:34:58.670 --> 00:35:01.430
I said the worst day of my life was
that day that I got the letter.

00:35:01.430 --> 00:35:04.142
This might have been the worst day of my,
this was a really hard day.

00:35:04.142 --> 00:35:09.700
My kids, my eldest who's 20,
was born in Minneapolis,

00:35:09.700 --> 00:35:11.860
when I was at Northwest, but
we moved when he was three weeks old.

00:35:11.860 --> 00:35:15.460
And the other two were born in Phoenix,
and that's where they were raised.

00:35:17.505 --> 00:35:22.380
&gt;&gt; And this merger was in
the papers all the time.

00:35:22.380 --> 00:35:26.847
One day my daughter,
who at the time I guess was,

00:35:26.847 --> 00:35:31.980
she's 15 now, so she was 13,
sends me a text and

00:35:31.980 --> 00:35:36.698
says Mrs. Roswell told me
the mergers going through [LAUGH].

00:35:36.698 --> 00:35:37.827
&gt;&gt; Who's Mrs. Roswell?

00:35:37.827 --> 00:35:40.220
&gt;&gt; Mrs. Roswell's a friend of a friend.

00:35:40.220 --> 00:35:44.645
Like, well,
I don't know before Mrs. Roswell.

00:35:44.645 --> 00:35:48.521
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I promise I'll tell you.

00:35:48.521 --> 00:35:52.976
But because things would happen like
a rumor that we got a deal done

00:35:52.976 --> 00:35:54.740
with the unions.

00:35:54.740 --> 00:35:57.340
And okay, the merge has gone
through is the way gets reported.

00:35:57.340 --> 00:36:00.470
So all of a sudden my kids
are just going crazy.

00:36:00.470 --> 00:36:02.590
So anyway they didn't want to move.

00:36:03.830 --> 00:36:05.990
So anyway, the bad part was
when we finally did do it, and

00:36:05.990 --> 00:36:08.770
I'd been telling them all along,
quit listening to everybody else.

00:36:08.770 --> 00:36:10.380
When I tell you is when it's happening.

00:36:10.380 --> 00:36:11.410
Don't worry about it until then.

00:36:11.410 --> 00:36:13.150
So they at least had some comfort in that.

00:36:13.150 --> 00:36:15.600
And then one day I had to come home and
Clara would say hey,

00:36:15.600 --> 00:36:17.360
I need to talk to you.

00:36:17.360 --> 00:36:22.400
And as soon as my daughter walked, I said
hey, I need to talk to you and pulled her.

00:36:22.400 --> 00:36:24.440
And we'd already had the two boys and
my wife there.

00:36:24.440 --> 00:36:27.060
And she walked in the room and
just saw that they were there and

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:28.540
just started sobbing.

00:36:28.540 --> 00:36:30.030
So, aren't you glad you
made me to tell that story?

00:36:30.030 --> 00:36:30.600
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry.

00:36:30.600 --> 00:36:31.560
&gt;&gt; It's all right.

00:36:31.560 --> 00:36:34.170
So no, it was really real, but
look, that's stuff you gotta do.

00:36:34.170 --> 00:36:34.917
She's fantastic.

00:36:34.917 --> 00:36:35.617
She's fine now.

00:36:35.617 --> 00:36:36.830
She wouldn't leave Dallas.

00:36:36.830 --> 00:36:37.920
So it's all fine.

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:40.970
But, yeah,
that was the bad part of doing this.

00:36:40.970 --> 00:36:45.740
And again, it gets back to, look,
if we were just doing this for ourselves,

00:36:45.740 --> 00:36:47.120
we would have stayed where we were.

00:36:47.120 --> 00:36:48.880
We were perfectly fine and happy.

00:36:48.880 --> 00:36:50.310
But you gotta do what's right for
the organization.

00:36:51.380 --> 00:36:55.010
&gt;&gt; And now that you're at this point,
the merger, the final pieces have come

00:36:55.010 --> 00:36:59.560
together, hopefully friends have
been made in Dallas, what's next for

00:36:59.560 --> 00:37:03.400
American as a company, and
what's next for you as CEO?

00:37:03.400 --> 00:37:06.850
&gt;&gt; Well, look,
we have set an objective for

00:37:06.850 --> 00:37:09.420
ourselves of becoming the greatest airline
in the world, and we're not there yet.

00:37:09.420 --> 00:37:11.580
So we have a lot of work to do.

00:37:11.580 --> 00:37:15.972
We spent the last two years integrating,
which sounds boring and it is.

00:37:15.972 --> 00:37:18.020
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But it's really hard work.

00:37:18.020 --> 00:37:19.250
And it takes a lot of time and

00:37:19.250 --> 00:37:22.420
effort, and our team has just
knocked the ball out of the park.

00:37:22.420 --> 00:37:25.420
But now we've gotten through
the vast majority of that.

00:37:25.420 --> 00:37:26.820
We still have some left.

00:37:26.820 --> 00:37:31.260
And now we really need go start producing
now that we are working as one airline.

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:32.350
And we know what we need to do.

00:37:32.350 --> 00:37:35.860
Our objective is to be
the best in the world and

00:37:35.860 --> 00:37:39.440
that's a somewhat nebulous term,
but we know we're not it now.

00:37:39.440 --> 00:37:42.340
So that at least we know we
have to make some progress, but

00:37:42.340 --> 00:37:45.590
what it certainly means is you gotta
be an airline that people want to fly.

00:37:45.590 --> 00:37:46.450
You have to be an airline
where people want to work, and

00:37:46.450 --> 00:37:52.730
you have to be an airline where
investors want to put their money.

00:37:52.730 --> 00:37:56.340
And we're doing okay on each of those,
but we can do a lot better on each.

00:37:56.340 --> 00:38:00.400
And we're not the best at any of them, and
we want to be the best at all of them.

00:38:00.400 --> 00:38:02.020
So we're going to make huge
improvements to the product.

00:38:02.020 --> 00:38:03.230
It's already begun.

00:38:03.230 --> 00:38:06.120
That includes the operational
liability issues that you mentioned.

00:38:06.120 --> 00:38:12.350
But it also includes entire modernization
of the fleet, brand new airplanes.

00:38:12.350 --> 00:38:15.610
We now have the youngest fleet in
the world, or in the United States.

00:38:15.610 --> 00:38:21.830
We have, that includes WiFi
throughout all our airplanes,

00:38:21.830 --> 00:38:24.170
including international,
lie-flat seats international.

00:38:24.170 --> 00:38:27.370
Taking our air miles clubs to look like
the kind of clubs you'd see when you

00:38:27.370 --> 00:38:29.850
fly internationally, not just when
you fly in the United States.

00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:32.120
So making it a true global airline
with a real global product.

00:38:32.120 --> 00:38:35.780
So we have a lot of work to do, and
we have a lot of work to do with our team.

00:38:35.780 --> 00:38:39.817
There's a lot of history at American
Airlines of management-labor relations

00:38:39.817 --> 00:38:41.680
that are just horrible.

00:38:41.680 --> 00:38:46.670
I'm not trying to say anything
bad about prior management.

00:38:46.670 --> 00:38:49.170
They had a really difficult
situation to go through.

00:38:49.170 --> 00:38:50.720
But the result was a really,

00:38:50.720 --> 00:38:55.390
really bad history that's hard for
people to let go of.

00:38:55.390 --> 00:38:59.020
And so we're trying to convince people
that that's over and this is new.

00:38:59.020 --> 00:39:01.640
And we try and
figure out ways to do that everyday,

00:39:01.640 --> 00:39:04.390
just surprise people with something
to say wow, that's different.

00:39:04.390 --> 00:39:05.730
This feels different.

00:39:05.730 --> 00:39:07.570
So we gotta lot of work
to do with out team, too.

00:39:07.570 --> 00:39:11.410
And by the way, it's incredibly
important to the first one.

00:39:12.490 --> 00:39:14.680
That the airline business is so

00:39:14.680 --> 00:39:18.720
much about the employees that you
interact with and their attitudes.

00:39:18.720 --> 00:39:22.100
So if we can get our employees to be
excited about being where they are,

00:39:22.100 --> 00:39:25.180
our customers will be much
happier on the airplane.

00:39:25.180 --> 00:39:28.188
And that'll help us drive more
passengers to the airline.

00:39:28.188 --> 00:39:32.070
And that'll help us meet our goals of
getting a return for our investors.

00:39:32.070 --> 00:39:33.740
So it starts with the employees.

00:39:33.740 --> 00:39:34.923
&gt;&gt; A lot of exciting work ahead.

00:39:34.923 --> 00:39:35.968
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:39:35.968 --> 00:39:38.789
&gt;&gt; Excellent, I'd like to open up
to the audience now for questions.

00:39:38.789 --> 00:39:40.947
I think our first one is from Twitter.

00:39:40.947 --> 00:39:41.563
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's right.

00:39:41.563 --> 00:39:44.880
Thanks both for a wonderful conversation.

00:39:44.880 --> 00:39:49.490
So the first question from Twitter comes
from a chap called Jake who says that

00:39:49.490 --> 00:39:53.680
the Economist wrote that airlines
are wonderful generators of profit for

00:39:53.680 --> 00:39:56.080
everyone except in sales.

00:39:56.080 --> 00:39:57.536
How do you envision changing that,

00:39:57.536 --> 00:40:00.165
especially with headwinds such
as climate change and so forth?

00:40:00.165 --> 00:40:06.631
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that was true from
the beginning of time until recently.

00:40:06.631 --> 00:40:11.008
Look, this airline,
this business, is transformed.

00:40:11.008 --> 00:40:16.725
And I believe that as much as I can
believe it, as much as I can possibly

00:40:16.725 --> 00:40:21.969
stress, no one not no one,
very few others seem to believe it.

00:40:21.969 --> 00:40:26.612
The market doesn't believe it, as you
can see with the way our stocks trading

00:40:26.612 --> 00:40:29.769
versus what we're looking
at in terms of earnings.

00:40:30.800 --> 00:40:32.594
It's hard for our employees to believe it.

00:40:32.594 --> 00:40:34.050
It's hard for
the management team to believe it.

00:40:34.050 --> 00:40:37.280
But this is, and it's obviously hard for
the economists to believe it but

00:40:37.280 --> 00:40:38.790
look, this is a real business now.

00:40:38.790 --> 00:40:40.588
We're producing real returns and

00:40:40.588 --> 00:40:43.939
we've got a business now that
it's still a cyclical business.

00:40:43.939 --> 00:40:48.537
People will fly less in bad times and
fly more in good times.

00:40:48.537 --> 00:40:53.142
But we're producing profits
now that are in line with

00:40:53.142 --> 00:40:58.079
the general industry,
actually a little ahead of them.

00:40:58.079 --> 00:41:03.260
Peaks, double the highest earnings
in the industry that are produced.

00:41:03.260 --> 00:41:06.990
Hopefully, we're not at
peak margins right now.

00:41:06.990 --> 00:41:10.310
So I think the industry has been
largely transformed mostly due

00:41:10.310 --> 00:41:15.270
to the consolidation that we discussed so
that's what we needed to do.

00:41:15.270 --> 00:41:18.344
The problem with the world before where
everyone else made money and we didn't.

00:41:18.344 --> 00:41:20.747
All of our suppliers made money,
Boeing made money.

00:41:20.747 --> 00:41:26.162
That's probably how America West and
others stayed around because

00:41:26.162 --> 00:41:31.577
those who lived off our carcass needed
us to be around a little more so

00:41:31.577 --> 00:41:35.390
they loaned us money to
keep us kicking around.

00:41:37.060 --> 00:41:40.270
On three occasions,
I've received loans from either GE or

00:41:40.270 --> 00:41:43.590
Airbus, to save a company
from going bankruptcy.

00:41:43.590 --> 00:41:47.130
We're going into bankruptcy,
America West and US Airways.

00:41:47.130 --> 00:41:48.350
But no one talks about that anymore.

00:41:48.350 --> 00:41:50.589
We're not even kind of doing that.

00:41:50.589 --> 00:41:57.046
We raised last week a billion dollars
of aircraft financing at below 4%.

00:41:57.046 --> 00:42:01.243
So anyway, the answer to that question if
you asked me five years ago or ten years

00:42:01.243 --> 00:42:05.520
ago was we got to get consolidation done
because the industry is too fragmented.

00:42:05.520 --> 00:42:09.411
And we can't provide to
consumers what they want,

00:42:09.411 --> 00:42:15.210
which is the ability to have one airline
take them everywhere they want to go.

00:42:15.210 --> 00:42:19.740
And as a result we all end up trying to do
that, and we're incredibly inefficient.

00:42:19.740 --> 00:42:21.250
So anyway, I think we're past it.

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:24.372
A lot of questions.

00:42:24.372 --> 00:42:25.869
&gt;&gt; There's mics going around.

00:42:25.869 --> 00:42:29.000
Thank you.

00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:29.892
&gt;&gt; Morning, thanks for coming.

00:42:29.892 --> 00:42:32.143
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; Really appreciated your remarks

00:42:32.143 --> 00:42:33.150
on teamwork.

00:42:33.150 --> 00:42:37.020
It made me think right away of
one of your agents in Raleigh,

00:42:37.020 --> 00:42:38.730
her name is Tammy Fields.

00:42:38.730 --> 00:42:40.205
We fly a ton.

00:42:40.205 --> 00:42:42.675
And she has basically adopted our family.

00:42:42.675 --> 00:42:46.545
She keeps an eye on like award seat
searches that we're trying to do to Tokyo

00:42:46.545 --> 00:42:49.655
or somewhere and
calls me up when something comes through.

00:42:49.655 --> 00:42:52.370
Just so far above and
beyond the call of duty.

00:42:52.370 --> 00:42:54.030
We were stranded one time in Peru.

00:42:54.030 --> 00:42:57.510
It was Tammy that got us out of
the fix and got us flights and

00:42:57.510 --> 00:42:59.520
pulled whatever strings
needed to be pulled.

00:42:59.520 --> 00:43:01.430
Really the kind of success
story you're looking for.

00:43:01.430 --> 00:43:03.466
I'd like to give you her name when
you're finished if that's okay.

00:43:03.466 --> 00:43:07.007
&gt;&gt; I've got it, and I see my colleague
down there typing it into his iPhone.

00:43:07.007 --> 00:43:08.899
We got Tammy Fields in Raleigh.

00:43:08.899 --> 00:43:10.187
&gt;&gt; Tammy Fields in Raleigh.
&gt;&gt; We will definitely let her know.

00:43:10.187 --> 00:43:11.058
&gt;&gt; Tammy Fields in Raleigh.

00:43:11.058 --> 00:43:11.906
&gt;&gt; Thank you for telling me.

00:43:11.906 --> 00:43:14.088
We do, we have a phenomenal
group of people like those.

00:43:14.088 --> 00:43:14.763
Thanks for telling me.

00:43:14.763 --> 00:43:21.673
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Hi,

00:43:21.673 --> 00:43:27.008
I wanted to ask about how you keep
loyal customers given the recent

00:43:27.008 --> 00:43:32.744
award program devaluation among
your competitors United and Delta.

00:43:32.744 --> 00:43:37.898
And American Americans impending
award devaluation of March 22nd.

00:43:37.898 --> 00:43:43.262
How do you look at customer loyalty and
maintain their trust and their business?

00:43:43.262 --> 00:43:45.525
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thanks.

00:43:45.525 --> 00:43:47.324
We don't view it as devaluation,
we view it as a change.

00:43:47.324 --> 00:43:52.965
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And look it is, these programs

00:43:54.350 --> 00:43:57.900
when they were established for
whatever reason, they were established as

00:43:59.890 --> 00:44:04.220
we measured loyalty by
how many miles you fly.

00:44:04.220 --> 00:44:06.670
And that's one way to do it, but we

00:44:09.200 --> 00:44:14.550
don't report our PNL as miles flown minus
expenses, it's revenues minus expenses.

00:44:14.550 --> 00:44:19.507
And what's more important and
what we want to reward for

00:44:19.507 --> 00:44:25.203
most people miles on revenues
are a reasonable approximation,

00:44:25.203 --> 00:44:31.123
but with that model you have these
very very odd things we created.

00:44:31.123 --> 00:44:36.490
People finding the lowest
possible fare they could find.

00:44:36.490 --> 00:44:41.582
And spending their weekends flying around
the lowest fares they could find so

00:44:41.582 --> 00:44:44.802
that they could get free
travel somewhere else.

00:44:44.802 --> 00:44:47.716
Look, we love those people,
those are customers of ours.

00:44:47.716 --> 00:44:52.607
But when that happens what we as
an airline are choosing to do is

00:44:52.607 --> 00:44:57.215
give that individual a first
class seat to Paris instead of

00:44:57.215 --> 00:45:00.903
the individual who didn't
fly as many miles.

00:45:00.903 --> 00:45:05.288
And actually spent his time and
weekends at home, but

00:45:05.288 --> 00:45:10.730
during the week paid full fair and
that's not the right model.

00:45:10.730 --> 00:45:12.200
So it's a shift in the model.

00:45:12.200 --> 00:45:14.430
It's one of these things again
where it's a transformation.

00:45:14.430 --> 00:45:17.350
So everyone that grew up in that old model
says that you're making this worse for

00:45:17.350 --> 00:45:21.350
me, but we're not making it worse for
everyone.

00:45:21.350 --> 00:45:23.070
It's worse for some, it's better for

00:45:23.070 --> 00:45:27.960
others and I think it's more fair and
more equitable.

00:45:27.960 --> 00:45:33.800
And it's rewarding what we want to reward,
which is those people who fly us,

00:45:33.800 --> 00:45:37.600
who pay the most.

00:45:37.600 --> 00:45:45.260
And who purchase the fares that
are not just going around and trying

00:45:45.260 --> 00:45:49.530
to find $100 fares Transcon on a weekend
to figure out how you get more miles.

00:45:49.530 --> 00:45:51.524
So again, it's great they do that.

00:45:51.524 --> 00:45:54.126
We encourage that by the model we had.

00:45:54.126 --> 00:45:57.870
We like to actually have those seats for

00:45:57.870 --> 00:46:03.099
the people that are producing
the most revenue for us.

00:46:03.099 --> 00:46:05.188
&gt;&gt; Hello, Tobias from Germany.

00:46:05.188 --> 00:46:10.010
I would like to talk about technology
because I haven't heard this at all today.

00:46:10.010 --> 00:46:14.600
I remember like 15 years ago I was
reading somewhere Internet in airplanes.

00:46:14.600 --> 00:46:15.377
I got very excited.

00:46:15.377 --> 00:46:18.082
I was very addictive to
Internet back then and

00:46:18.082 --> 00:46:23.277
then it took 15 years until at least in
Europe it was most of the airplanes a and

00:46:23.277 --> 00:46:26.580
even now EasyJet right now,
Lufthansa just started.

00:46:26.580 --> 00:46:30.310
It took like 15 years until
people were able to use Internet.

00:46:30.310 --> 00:46:33.990
In US, it was a bit earlier but
it was still taking a long time.

00:46:33.990 --> 00:46:38.210
So my question is like how in the future
you can guarantee that technology is being

00:46:38.210 --> 00:46:40.800
integrated much earlier into airplanes,

00:46:40.800 --> 00:46:43.141
especially in regards
of customer experience.

00:46:43.141 --> 00:46:46.353
Because I would love to have all
within American Airlines app I

00:46:46.353 --> 00:46:49.750
could upgrade myself during a plane,
maybe for a special price,

00:46:49.750 --> 00:46:53.952
business class where maybe could skip the
line or maybe get fast boarding because

00:46:53.952 --> 00:46:56.887
there's no large queue and
I feel like I'm just tired.

00:46:56.887 --> 00:46:59.375
I really feel like
the customer experience,

00:46:59.375 --> 00:47:01.992
which a lot of online
companies are offering and

00:47:01.992 --> 00:47:05.539
the mobile technologies out there
has arrived at the airlines?

00:47:05.539 --> 00:47:07.090
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:47:08.320 --> 00:47:12.160
Look, we're moving as fast
as we possibly can and

00:47:12.160 --> 00:47:17.260
because of customer demand, because of
people like you, who are insisting.

00:47:17.260 --> 00:47:22.910
The reality is Wi-Fi on aircraft
now that you talked about

00:47:22.910 --> 00:47:27.780
there I remember Russ sitting around and
debating it and jeez, there's

00:47:27.780 --> 00:47:30.820
no way you can possibly pay for yourself,
you gotta carry this stuff around.

00:47:30.820 --> 00:47:34.406
These are antennas that communicate
down the cell towers and

00:47:34.406 --> 00:47:38.634
they're really expensive and
they're heavy and people got it moved.

00:47:38.634 --> 00:47:41.730
There's no way we're going to,
people pay for it.

00:47:41.730 --> 00:47:44.940
But once it was on the airplane, on a few,

00:47:44.940 --> 00:47:48.500
customers started saying,
this airplane doesn't have Wi-Fi anymore?

00:47:48.500 --> 00:47:51.364
So there's no choice,
because we needed to have Internet.

00:47:51.364 --> 00:47:56.526
It came really quickly, so
I don't know if that was so

00:47:56.526 --> 00:48:01.693
much technology as maybe
resistance from airlines to

00:48:01.693 --> 00:48:06.767
go do something that we
couldn't cause just as one.

00:48:06.767 --> 00:48:10.404
But that's all behind us now and
this is going so fast,

00:48:10.404 --> 00:48:14.372
we're all trying to figure how
it lead from somebody else.

00:48:14.372 --> 00:48:19.217
As it relates to some of the things
you're talk about we're working

00:48:19.217 --> 00:48:24.062
diligently to figure out everything
we can to make sure the customer

00:48:24.062 --> 00:48:28.660
experience can be done as much
through technology as possible.

00:48:28.660 --> 00:48:32.596
Some of the things you describe we just
simply can't do because the regulations we

00:48:32.596 --> 00:48:33.519
have to live with.

00:48:33.519 --> 00:48:37.630
I can tell you right now,
if the way I travel and

00:48:37.630 --> 00:48:42.903
the way probably most of you travel,
there's no reason for

00:48:42.903 --> 00:48:47.460
me to see an individual
until I get on the airplane.

00:48:47.460 --> 00:48:50.460
There's no reason to
have an interaction or

00:48:50.460 --> 00:48:53.650
even to see an American Airlines
employee until I get,

00:48:53.650 --> 00:48:58.230
because I'm not checking a bag and
I've got my boarding pass, my iPhone,

00:48:58.230 --> 00:49:03.518
and I hand it to someone, who then puts
it under the thing, and I can do that.

00:49:03.518 --> 00:49:06.505
But we're not allowed to not
have an agent stand there, and

00:49:06.505 --> 00:49:10.105
just let an individual decide that
they're going to get on the airplane.

00:49:10.105 --> 00:49:14.140
So some of this is just requirements
that we're always going to have, but

00:49:14.140 --> 00:49:17.446
we can do a lot better and
we're working really hard at it.

00:49:17.446 --> 00:49:21.548
I can tell you American Airlines
as it relates to this,

00:49:21.548 --> 00:49:26.350
we're putting Internet onto
our international flights.

00:49:26.350 --> 00:49:30.490
It's not on all of them yet, but
we're way ahead of the game there.

00:49:30.490 --> 00:49:32.740
So trans Atlantic,
things you couldn't do before.

00:49:32.740 --> 00:49:37.330
Now we're shooting up satellites
instead of going down to cell towers.

00:49:37.330 --> 00:49:42.635
And with that technology that's new
technology, now we can actually provide

00:49:42.635 --> 00:49:49.270
Wi-Fi service anywhere in the air, so
we're moving to that as quickly as we can.

00:49:49.270 --> 00:49:52.450
So we'll keep working on it but
we agree with you.

00:49:52.450 --> 00:49:57.560
&gt;&gt; I'm afraid I'm going to take the last
question for myself, which thanks.

00:49:57.560 --> 00:49:58.910
Which is-
&gt;&gt; You earned it.

00:49:58.910 --> 00:50:00.955
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Thank you, I've tried.

00:50:00.955 --> 00:50:01.607
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:50:01.607 --> 00:50:04.250
&gt;&gt; What advice do you have for
the MBAs in the room?

00:50:04.250 --> 00:50:07.131
If you could go back and
it's your time at Owen.

00:50:07.131 --> 00:50:09.733
What do you wish you could tell yourself?

00:50:09.733 --> 00:50:17.020
&gt;&gt; Again, so many of your would go off and
live in your basements.

00:50:17.020 --> 00:50:18.837
So I'm not sure what to tell you about.

00:50:18.837 --> 00:50:23.017
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; About how to do that.

00:50:23.017 --> 00:50:24.680
You're smarter than I am.

00:50:24.680 --> 00:50:30.010
But what I can tell you is what I do
know at least from a more traditional

00:50:30.010 --> 00:50:36.420
perspective, where you
go show up at a job.

00:50:36.420 --> 00:50:41.360
I was honored that's part of
the introduction to have this

00:50:41.360 --> 00:50:43.550
the relationships matter piece mentioned.

00:50:45.180 --> 00:50:49.712
That's what I think, what I believe is and
what I would encourage,

00:50:49.712 --> 00:50:53.209
the reason I was talking
to everybody like this, and

00:50:53.209 --> 00:50:56.728
what I told them was what I
have learned in my career.

00:50:56.728 --> 00:51:00.172
I know it's taught at business schools and
it seems so

00:51:00.172 --> 00:51:05.160
matter of fact that why do you even teach
it, but you can't stress it enough.

00:51:05.160 --> 00:51:09.091
It's how important relationships are and

00:51:09.091 --> 00:51:13.469
how you treat people in
your relative success.

00:51:13.469 --> 00:51:20.793
And there's this perception amongst some
outside of business that business is this,

00:51:20.793 --> 00:51:28.040
the way you get ahead is backstabbing,
cutthroat that doesn't work at all.

00:51:28.040 --> 00:51:31.670
It worked for a short period of time but
you'll be done and you'll go need to find

00:51:31.670 --> 00:51:35.460
another job before too long in another
industry because it doesn't work.

00:51:35.460 --> 00:51:41.453
What matters is people that in
every relationship they have,

00:51:41.453 --> 00:51:48.956
you treat people with respect and dignity
and you show up and do your job well.

00:51:48.956 --> 00:51:53.612
I watch people and I don't think they
fully appreciate is, from the time you

00:51:53.612 --> 00:51:58.905
walk in the door that starts, and
you're building relationships with people.

00:51:58.905 --> 00:52:03.780
And you don't have any idea,
you can't possibly decide

00:52:03.780 --> 00:52:07.390
that's the person somebody really need to
have a relationship and that person's not.

00:52:07.390 --> 00:52:10.020
That's what I've learned through
all this crazy story of all

00:52:10.020 --> 00:52:11.040
the stuff I've been through.

00:52:11.040 --> 00:52:13.365
This American Airlines, US Airways merger,

00:52:13.365 --> 00:52:16.654
we ended up with people in part of
this process that helped us out.

00:52:16.654 --> 00:52:21.514
That 15 years ago, we worked on
this project and at that time,

00:52:21.514 --> 00:52:27.544
there's no way we would have thought that
individual was going to be representing

00:52:27.544 --> 00:52:32.590
the flight attendants who are really
important to us at that time.

00:52:32.590 --> 00:52:35.626
But thank goodness we treated
that person well, 15 years ago.

00:52:35.626 --> 00:52:39.882
And if they hadn't been treated maybe so
well from the other side,

00:52:39.882 --> 00:52:42.320
it made a huge difference.

00:52:42.320 --> 00:52:43.530
So anyway, the message is this.

00:52:43.530 --> 00:52:44.162
You're going to,

00:52:44.162 --> 00:52:47.015
from the day you walk in the door you're
going to start these relationships.

00:52:47.015 --> 00:52:51.683
And if you try and say I care
about that person not that person,

00:52:51.683 --> 00:52:54.000
that's not going to work.

00:52:54.000 --> 00:52:55.160
So treat everybody the same.

00:52:55.160 --> 00:53:00.023
Treat everybody, and if you do that
the people that do that versus those

00:53:00.023 --> 00:53:02.871
who don't, do it dramatically better.

00:53:02.871 --> 00:53:04.630
And I think that's
the biggest key as anything.

00:53:04.630 --> 00:53:09.890
So you give somebody the same intellect,
energy, integrity, all that, but

00:53:09.890 --> 00:53:14.900
not the ability to build relationships
with somebody who can, and

00:53:14.900 --> 00:53:16.780
they're going to have
dramatically different careers.

00:53:16.780 --> 00:53:20.623
So that would be my advice,
just be nice to people.

00:53:20.623 --> 00:53:22.714
&gt;&gt; That's great advice-
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:53:22.714 --> 00:53:23.970
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much Doug.

00:53:23.970 --> 00:53:24.622
It's been amazing.

00:53:24.622 --> 00:53:32.286
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:53:32.286 --> 00:53:36.720
[MUSIC]

